Cat-eye gel polish is a magnetic-infused gel that, once cured under a handheld magnet instead of a lamp first, forms a single shifting line of light down the center of the nail. The mechanism is genuinely different from chrome: metallic particles suspended in the gel align toward the magnet before the gel sets, then the whole thing cures under UV or LED light to lock the pattern in place. Hold the magnet too far from the nail and the line comes out faint. Cure before holding the magnet close enough, and the effect does not form at all, it is the single most common mistake first-timers make.

Both techniques photograph beautifully but wear differently, and worth booking either only after you have decided which finish matches your maintenance tolerance. Chrome shows fingerprint smudging faster in the first 48 hours. Cat-eye holds its shine longer but shows tip wear a few days sooner than a standard glossy gel. Neither is the wrong choice. They are just solving for different things. If your base color decisions usually start with the shade card, <a href='/blog/september/september-nail-colors'>september nail colors</a> is worth a look before you commit to which metallic direction fits your existing rotation.

