## Glass, Jelly, and Glazed Finishes Making a Comeback

A glass or jelly finish is a translucent gel polish applied in thin, sheer layers so the natural nail shows through, closer to stained glass than a solid opaque coat. It sits at the opposite end of the texture spectrum from matte: instead of killing shine, it is built entirely around depth and light transmission.

This finish disappeared for a couple of seasons while chrome and cat-eye took over the metallic conversation, and its return this September is less a fad revival than a direct reaction to it. After a run of heavy, opaque metallics, a sheer, glassy manicure reads as the palate cleanser. The technique itself is simple in concept, thin coats built up gradually rather than one or two opaque layers, but it rewards patience more than any other finish in this list. Rush the layers and the color pools unevenly at the cuticle.

Glazed donut-adjacent finishes fall into this same family, using a milky, translucent top coat over a barely-there base to mimic that soft-focus, glossy-blur effect. It is one of the few looks here that genuinely splits opinion between "elevated minimalism" and "unfinished," and which side you land on says more about personal taste than the technique's actual execution quality.

